---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import { withBase } from '../../lib/paths';

const workflowYml = `# ---
# title: My Workflow
# description: One sentence on what it does.
# tags:
#   - ci
# icon: bolt
# owner: your-org
# ---
---
name: my-workflow
files:
  - source: my-workflow.yml
    target: .github/workflows/my-workflow.yml`;

const packageJson = `{
  "name": "@ghwfxlab/my-workflow",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "files": ["my-workflow.yml", "workflow.yml"],
  "publishConfig": { "registry": "https://npm.pkg.github.com" }
}`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Writing a package"
  description="How to add a workflow package to the GHWM registry: directory layout, manifests, readme, and publishing."
  current="writing-a-package"
>
  <p>
    A package is one directory under <code>workflows/</code> in the registry repository. Merging it to
    <code>main</code> publishes it automatically. Read the
    <a href={withBase('/docs/manifest/')}>manifest reference</a> for the full list of fields.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Create the directory</h2>
  <CodeBlock code="mkdir workflows/my-workflow" />

  <h2>2. Add the workflow file</h2>
  <p>
    Name it <code>my-workflow.yml</code> (or <code>.yaml</code>). The filename must match the directory name.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Add <code>workflow.yml</code></h2>
  <p>List every file the CLI should write into consumer repositories.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={workflowYml} label="workflows/my-workflow/workflow.yml" />

  <h2>4. Add <code>package.json</code></h2>
  <CodeBlock code={packageJson} label="workflows/my-workflow/package.json" />

  <h2>5. Write the readme</h2>
  <p>Add <code>README.md</code> in the package directory. It must cover:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>What the workflow does, in one paragraph</li>
    <li>What gets installed in the consumer repository</li>
    <li>Setup steps: secrets, variables, and permissions</li>
    <li>How to trigger the workflow</li>
    <li>How to update and remove it</li>
    <li>Known limitations</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>6. Register it</h2>
  <p>
    Add a row to the Available Workflows table in both <code>README.md</code> and
    <code>workflows/README.md</code>. The root <code>package.json</code> uses
    <code>workspaces: ["workflows/*"]</code>, so the new directory is picked up automatically.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Open a pull request</h2>
  <p>
    Use a <a href="https://www.conventionalcommits.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Conventional Commits</a>
    title such as <code>feat(my-workflow): add my-workflow</code>, and run <code>make lang</code> and
    <code>make precommit</code> first.
  </p>

  <h2>Versioning and publishing</h2>
  <p>
    On merge to <code>main</code>, the publish workflow compares each package's version with GitHub Packages and
    publishes only versions that are new. A pre-commit hook bumps the patch version when shipped content
    changed. Bump <code>package.json</code> yourself for a minor or major release.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Patch</strong>: bugfixes, no behaviour change</li>
    <li><strong>Minor</strong>: new optional inputs, additive features, new packaged config files</li>
    <li><strong>Major</strong>: breaking changes such as renamed files, removed inputs, changed permissions or default triggers</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Only <code>package.json</code> and the paths in its <code>files</code> array count as shipped content, so
    readme-only changes do not need a bump.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
